<section class="page-head section section--tight">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">Common question</p>
    <h1>Is RCPD dangerous?</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      <strong>No &mdash; RCPD (retrograde cricopharyngeal dysfunction), or no-burp syndrome, is
      not considered dangerous or life-threatening.</strong> Trapped air is uncomfortable and
      often genuinely painful, but it has not been shown to cause lasting damage to the
      esophagus, stomach or any other organ. The real cost of RCPD is to quality of life.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap split split--2">
    <div class="panel">
      <h2>What actually happens</h2>
      <p>
        When you swallow, a little air goes down with the food. Normally the cricopharyngeus
        muscle at the top of the esophagus relaxes and lets that air rise back up as a burp. In
        RCPD it stays shut.
      </p>
      <p>
        The air does not simply keep accumulating. It moves down through the digestive tract and
        leaves the other way. That is why the condition produces bloating, loud gurgling from the
        chest and throat, and a great deal of flatulence &mdash; rather than injury.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="panel panel--tint">
      <h2>What it does affect</h2>
      <p>Being unable to burp is not harmless in the way that matters day to day. People with RCPD commonly report:</p>
      <ul class="stack">
        <li>Chronic bloating and chest or upper-abdomen pressure</li>
        <li>Avoiding beer, soda, sparkling water and large meals</li>
        <li>Anxiety around eating out, dating, and social events</li>
        <li>Embarrassment about audible gurgling and flatulence</li>
        <li>Years of being told it is reflux, IBS, or stress</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tight">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section__head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Reassurance</p>
      <h2>Is no-burp syndrome life-threatening?</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        No. The pressure RCPD creates sits high in the chest and it can be intense, so a lot of
        people assume something is wrong with their heart or their esophagus. Many end up having
        normal endoscopies, ECGs or barium swallows before anyone mentions the cricopharyngeus.
      </p>
      <p>
        Those normal results are not a dead end &mdash; they are part of the pattern. RCPD
        typically does not show up on standard tests, which is exactly why it went unrecognized
        for so long. A specialist usually identifies it from the symptom history instead.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tight">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section__head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Important</p>
      <h2>When should I see a doctor?</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="callout callout--note">
      <p>
        <strong>When to get checked anyway.</strong> RCPD being benign does not mean every
        symptom should be assumed to be RCPD. See a doctor promptly if you have trouble
        swallowing food or liquids, unexplained weight loss, persistent vomiting, vomiting blood,
        or black or tarry stools &mdash; these are not features of RCPD and point to something
        else that deserves attention. <strong>Severe or crushing chest pain, particularly if it
        spreads to the jaw or arm or comes with breathlessness, is a medical emergency &mdash;
        call your local emergency number rather than waiting.</strong>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section__head">
      <p class="eyebrow">The other worry</p>
      <h2>Does RCPD get worse over time?</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        RCPD behaves like a lifelong, stable trait rather than a progressive disease. Most people
        describe having had it for as long as they can remember, and the
        <a href="/research.html">published research</a> does not describe the underlying muscle
        problem deteriorating with age.
      </p>
      <p>
        Symptoms can certainly feel worse in some periods than others &mdash; carbonated drinks,
        rich or rushed meals, and stress all make trapped air more noticeable &mdash; but that is
        the load changing, not the condition advancing.
      </p>
      <p>
        And it is treatable. A botulinum toxin injection into the cricopharyngeus relaxes the
        muscle so air can escape, and the
        <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11474230/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2024
        systematic review of 637&nbsp;patients</a> found it to be effective, with cricopharyngeal
        myotomy available when injections are not enough. Many people burp for the first time in
        their lives within days.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

